{
  "id": "bigram-probabilities",
  "title": "Bigram Probabilities (Add-1 Smoothing)",
  "description": "Build a bigram language model with add-one smoothing. Sort the unique tokens to obtain vocabulary vocab. Row $i$ represents context token vocab[i], and column $j$ represents next token vocab[j].\n\n$$\nP(w_j\\mid w_i)=\\frac{C_{ij}+1}{\\sum_{u=1}^{V}C_{iu}+V}\n$$\n\nHere, $C_{ij}$ is the number of adjacent occurrences of $w_i$ followed by $w_j$, and $V$ is vocabulary size. Return vocab as a list, counts as an integer NumPy matrix, and probabilities as a floating-point NumPy matrix in a dictionary.",
  "seoDescription": "Estimate bigram probabilities from token sequences using add-one smoothing over a fixed vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: tokens = [\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"]\n\nOutput: {\"vocab\": [\"a\", \"b\"], \"counts\": [[0, 1], [1, 0]], \"probabilities\": [[0.333333, 0.666667], [0.666667, 0.333333]]}\n\nExplanation: The observed transitions are a to b and b to a; add-one smoothing also assigns probability to unseen pairs.",
    "Input: tokens = [\"i\", \"love\", \"ml\", \"love\", \"ml\"]\n\nOutput: {\"vocab\": [\"i\", \"love\", \"ml\"], \"counts\": [[0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 2], [0, 1, 0]], \"probabilities\": [[0.25, 0.5, 0.25], [0.2, 0.2, 0.6], [0.25, 0.5, 0.25]]}"
  ],
  "requirements": "* Sort the unique corpus tokens to define row and column order\n* Count every adjacent token pair in the count matrix\n* Apply add-one smoothing independently to each context row\n* Return exactly vocab, counts, and probabilities in a dictionary",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Bigram Probabilities (Add-1 Smoothing)\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of variable-length sequences of discrete tokens with complex grammatical and long-range semantic dependencies. Modern Transformer architectures eliminate sequential recurrent bottlenecks by allowing every token to compute dynamic affinity scores with every other token. **Bigram Probabilities (Add-1 Smoothing)** is an essential sequence modeling operation in modern large language models.\n\nIn this problem, the objective is to implement **Bigram Probabilities (Add-1 Smoothing)** efficiently and accurately adhering to standard mathematical conventions.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe operation is governed by the following mathematical formulation:\n\n$$\nP(w_j\\mid w_i)=\\frac{C_{ij}+1}{\\sum_{u=1}^{V}C_{iu}+V}\n$$\n\nEvery variable and index in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1: Input Validation & Pre-processing**: Validate tensor dimensions, convert inputs into contiguous array buffers, and extract structural shape parameters.\n2. **Phase 2: Core Transformation**: Apply vectorized operations directly across array dimensions without explicit Python loops.\n3. **Phase 3: Aggregation & Normalization**: Compute required summations, projections, or element-wise bounds while safeguarding against numerical instability.\n4. **Phase 4: Output Formatting**: Ensure the resulting array or dictionary matches expected output data types and precision tolerances.\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **All-to-All Self-Attention**: Dot-product attention computes contextualized representations dynamically, weighting relevant context tokens higher than uninformative tokens.\n* **Causal Masking**: In autoregressive decoder models (like GPT and LLaMA), upper-triangular masking ensures the model cannot look ahead at future tokens during generation.\n* **Positional Embeddings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings inject sequence order into the token representations.",
